I get tired of rebuilding and reinstalling my web server every time I want to add in a new module, or when I decide to take one out, because I never really use it. This is where shared objects are handy. A shared o
bject is something that gets loaded dynamically by a process when it needs it. This saves you from having to compile that code into the program executable, which, in turn, makes the executable smaller, and load up faster. By making your Apache modules int
o shared objects, you can build everything into your server, but only actually use the parts that you want at any one time, and leave out everything else that you're not using.
\par On Windows, these are things called ``dynamic link libraries'', or DLLs. On Linux, they are called shared objects, or .so files.
\par In order to enable shared objects, you have to compile a module called }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 mod_so}{, which, in turn, loads all the modules that you have compiled as shared objects. }{
\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 mod_so}{ itself cannot be shared object, of course, because there would be no way to load it. Chicken, egg.
\par So, to build your Apache server to use shared objects, run the following commands:
\par }\pard\plain \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 
\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\par }\pard \s18\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 
        ./configure --prefix=/path/to/apache \\
\par \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2                   --enable-module=most \\
\par \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2                   --enable\hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 -shared=max
\par }\pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs20\i (You can either literally type those \\
 characters, or just put this command all on one line and omit the \\'s)}{
\par What does this command do? Well, it compiles all of the modules that ship with Apache, except those that are considered experimental or unstable, and enables them all as DSO's.
\par This means that Apache will be loading up a bunch of modules that you don't really want, so you need to edit the configuration file and comment out those modules that you're not really going to use.
\par But it also means that if you want to add in a particular module, you can do so by putting it in the configuration file, rather than having to recompile your server from source.
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\pict{\*\picprop\shplid1028{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}
{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fillColor}{\sv 8421504}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLine}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn alignHR}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn dxHeightHR}{\sv 30}}{\sp{\sn fStandardHR}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fHorizRule}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}
\picscalex1287\picscaley6\piccropl0\piccropr0\piccropt0\piccropb0\picw1764\pich882\picwgoal1000\pichgoal500\wmetafile8}}{
\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs48\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning36\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart configuring}Configuring
{\*\bkmkend configuring}
\par }\pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {Once you've compiled and installed your server, you need to configure 
it for your particular environment. Many of the configuration directives got set when you ran }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 configure}{ (or }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 Configure}{
), and so the server should work correctly immediately. However, you will probably want to change some things, since the default installation is very generic, and not precisely suited to your needs.
\par Apache, unlike most of its competitors in the web server market, lets you configure everything, down to the smallest detail. And if there's really something that you want to configure that you can
't, you have the source code, so you can change it if you are so inclined.
\par }\pard\plain \s2\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs36\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart configuration_files}Configuration files
{\*\bkmkend configuration_files}
\par }\pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {The configuration for your Apache server is located in a file called }{
\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 httpd.conf}{, which is usually located at }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 /usr/local/apache/conf/httpd.conf}{.
