\par }\pard\plain \s1\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel0\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \b\fs48\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning36\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkstart ssl_and_ecommerce}SSL and e-commerce
{\*\bkmkend ssl_and_ecommerce}
\par }\pard\plain \s17\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
Even better than telling your customers about your business, is actually doing business online. You can put your catalog online, and let customers order, and pay for, your merchandise directly on your web site.
\par The one problem is that people are rather picky about who they give their credit card information to. And they are extrememly reluctant to type it in and submit it across the Internet without some assurance that what they are doing is secure.
\par By default, data that is passed to web servers is ``in the clear'', meaning that it is not encrypted, and anyone that is watching the wire would be able to see anything that went past. Like, for example, y
our credit card number. Even when you are in a password protected area on a web site, the username and password, and any data exchanged, is all passed in the clear.
\par One way around this is }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 SSL}{. }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 SSL}{, which stands for }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 
Secure Socket Layers}{, is a technology tha
t encrypts traffic between the server and the client using a private key/public key technique. That means that only the people on the two ends can understand it. And even if someone were to intercept the entire message, they would not be able to decrypt i
t.
\par There are a number of SSL implementations that run on top of Apache. Two of the better known ones are Raven, from Covalent (http://www.covalent.com/raven/ssl/), and Stronghold (http://www.c2.net/products/sh2/). These are both commercial products.
\par The Open Source alternative is }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 mod_ssl}{ (http://www.modssl.org/), which runs in conjunction with }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 OpenSSL}{
 (http://www.openssl.org).
\par With the recent changes in the crypto laws, there's a good chance that }{\cs19\fs20\loch\af2\hich\af2\dbch\af2 \hich\af2\dbch\af2\loch\f2 mod_ssl}{ will ship as one of the standard Apache modules in future releases.
